From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 09:20:14 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 42/90] Plutonium: printing in tabs goes to the shell

EXO-TAB-FEATURES (docs/features/TAB_FEATURES.md, "Printing"): the
registry's tabs had no PrintViewManager, so Ctrl+P, window.print() and
the context menu's Print did nothing.

- Tab::AttachHelpers() attaches printing (InitializePrintingForWebContents:
  PrintViewManager, PrintCompositeClient) and a WebContentsModalDialogManager
  whose host is the window of the shell that would be asked about the tab.
- PrintViewManager::SetPreviewOverride(): where PrintViewManager would open
  Chrome's preview (a user's print, or a scripted one), plutonium_print.cc
  asks the shell instead, as a tab request of kind "print" with {title,
  url, thread, hasSelection, scripted, pdf}. The answer, "done" or
  "cancel" (from the shell or an agent), ends the preview: the page gets
  "afterprint" and window.print() returns. If no shell takes it, Chrome's
  preview opens over the shell's window; if the shell that took it goes
  away, printing is cancelled.
- Commands: "print" (Ctrl+P, as Chrome's Print command) and "print-to-pdf"
  (Page.printToPDF's options, in inches; {data: base64}), which prints the
  frame that asked while a request is open, for the shell's preview and
  for printing through the helper. A renderer can't print while it is in
  a print preview (it stops responding), so the request's first PDF ends
  the preview there: the page gets "afterprint" and window.print()
  returns then, while the request stays open until it is answered.
  print-to-pdf refuses while Chrome's own preview is open.
- A tab's context menu keeps Print.
